1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,487
This interim comparison examines Zeron and TerminalX in separate fresh macOS virtual
machines created from the same Lume base.

2
00:00:07,487 --> 00:00:15,308
Zeron was exercised directly. TerminalX was built and launched, but its desktop
walkthrough is pending a host Accessibility repair.

3
00:00:15,308 --> 00:00:23,842
The video distinguishes observed behavior from source-confirmed features. Narration
is synthetic, and no successful authenticated agent run is claimed.

4
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:31,403
Zeron starts with a guided sequence: choose an appearance, decide whether to sync,
and install an agent.

5
00:00:31,403 --> 00:00:36,303
The sync step can be skipped, so local use does not require an application account.

6
00:00:36,303 --> 00:00:46,985
The sequence is clear, but a microphone permission prompt appeared before I asked to
use voice. TerminalX should adopt helpful setup guidance while requesting
permissions only at the point of use.

7
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:54,977
In the clean Zeron VM, clicking Install beside Codex started an inline installation
and then marked the provider available.

8
00:00:54,977 --> 00:01:02,870
This is a concrete onboarding advantage. TerminalX currently detects existing
command-line agents and links out to installation instructions.

9
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:09,196
The opportunity is a guided installation and login handoff with progress,
cancellation and a reliable re-check.

10
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:17,285
The composer keeps the project, device, model, reasoning level, branch and checkout
choice close to the prompt.

11
00:01:17,285 --> 00:01:21,834
Choosing a new worktree makes isolation an explicit decision before work starts.

12
00:01:21,834 --> 00:01:33,572
TerminalX already has these kinds of controls. The improvement is to group them
around the questions a user must answer: which agent, where it runs, what files it
changes, and which permissions apply.

13
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:42,428
The first prompt created a real Git worktree. A terminal opened in that checkout,
and the print-working-directory command confirmed the location.

14
00:01:42,428 --> 00:01:48,319
Because Codex had no account credentials, the chat eventually showed a signed-out
error with a login action.

15
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:57,001
This exposes an opportunity for both products: distinguish installed from
authenticated before a run begins, and preserve the draft when setup is needed.

16
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:04,494
Zeron combines files, Markdown preview, terminal, diffs and browser surfaces in the
workspace.

17
00:02:04,494 --> 00:02:13,176
This diff shows three lines deliberately added by the test operator, not by an
agent. The working-tree scope and added-line count make the result understandable.

18
00:02:13,176 --> 00:02:21,082
TerminalX already supports rich changes and editor views. Prioritize clear scope,
provenance and a stable way to open each tool.

19
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:29,143
I loaded a public page inside Zeron and switched the workspace to dark appearance.
The embedded page followed that appearance.

20
00:02:29,143 --> 00:02:34,568
Settings also expose accents, material choices and distinct font controls in source.

21
00:02:34,568 --> 00:02:44,924
TerminalX has themes, adjustable scale, a browser and an editor already. Better
discoverability and measured readability are stronger priorities than simply adding
more decoration.

22
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:53,900
Zeron exposes Appshots as a user-triggered context-capture workflow, with a
shortcut, destination and permission explanation.

23
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:00,286
The setup screen promises review before sending. I did not enable capture or
validate that workflow end to end.

24
00:03:00,286 --> 00:03:09,446
TerminalX could connect its floating chat and attachment support into a similar
capture-to-draft experience while preserving protected windows and human-only
confirmations.

25
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:18,536
Zeron defines ten production agent harness identities in the inspected release. Only
Codex was installed and attempted in this walkthrough.

26
00:03:18,536 --> 00:03:25,130
TerminalX deliberately offers Claude Code and Codex for new work, with legacy Cursor
and OpenCode adapters hidden.

27
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:36,327
Broader provider support is a product decision. Each additional provider needs
reliable authentication, status, interruption, resume and permission behavior, not
just a new picker entry.

28
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:44,526
TerminalX was built from the comparison branch and installed in a second fresh
clone, without Zeron data or installed agents.

29
00:03:44,526 --> 00:03:52,685
Its command-line interface confirmed version zero point two point nineteen, an
active local runtime, zero projects and zero sessions.

30
00:03:52,685 --> 00:04:04,214
The host computer-use helper then refused the new Lume window. A human Accessibility
repair is required, so the remaining TerminalX discussion is based on source rather
than a completed desktop walkthrough.

31
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:14,891
TerminalX gives chat and terminal views of the same real agent command-line process.
That is a different interaction model from Zeron’s structured Codex harness and
separate shell.

32
00:04:14,891 --> 00:04:22,356
TerminalX also provides a cross-project attention dashboard and an issue-to-pull-
request workflow with independent review and merge checks.

33
00:04:22,356 --> 00:04:29,320
These are strengths to preserve. A competing pull-request viewer should not be
treated as equivalent to a gated review pipeline.

34
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:39,543
Start with guided installation and a truthful readiness state. Then make application
login, provider login and execution location unmistakably different.

35
00:04:39,543 --> 00:04:44,531
Group launch controls around user decisions, and keep the effective permission mode
visible.

36
00:04:44,531 --> 00:04:54,385
A compact current-plan tray could complement the existing attention dashboard.
Prompt delivery and recovery should also say exactly what was confirmed and what
action is needed next.

37
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:02,590
The next increment should make existing tools easier to find: files, terminal,
browser, changes and history.

38
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:12,211
Add clearer diff scope, searchable settings and notification previews. Validate
contrast, font scaling, keyboard access and narrow layouts with measurements.

39
00:05:12,211 --> 00:05:20,976
Capture-to-draft is a distinct product opportunity. Provider expansion and theme
imports should follow demonstrated demand rather than a feature-count contest.

40
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:31,614
The accompanying report contains a detailed feature matrix, fourteen prioritized
improvements, acceptance criteria, screenshots and reproduction notes.

41
00:05:31,614 --> 00:05:37,918
TypeScript checks, all three thousand three hundred eighty-seven tests, and the
TerminalX Dev build passed.

42
00:05:37,918 --> 00:05:48,739
The work remains incomplete until the TerminalX desktop walkthrough is recorded.
Authenticated agent execution, sync, cloud compute and voice also remain explicitly
unverified.
